detailed_description: <Video 1> is the persistent LOCATION REFERENCE for the static environment and spatial layout (The Stone Hearth Tavern is a rectangular stone hall measuring eighty feet east‑west and sixty feet north‑south, with thick stone walls that creak under the wooden floor. The south wall hosts the main entrance: a single back door centered on the wall, its wooden latch protruding from the rear to allow inward swing. A clear aisle at least twelve feet wide runs from this doorway straight north toward the hall’s northern edge. Along the east side of the hall sits the counter—a three‑foot‑wide bar that stretches twenty feet in length. Its centreline lies ten feet north of the south wall and fifteen feet west of the eastern wall, leaving ample space for patrons to move around. The polished tables are set along this counter; each is four by four feet and fixed at their positions. Near the back end of the counter a high bar stool stands beside a shelf of crystal cups that rises six feet tall and three feet deep. On the west wall sits the hearth, occupying a ten‑by‑twelve‑foot stone area centred fifteen feet northward from the south wall. Beside this hearth an upright barrel—eight feet high with a four‑foot diameter—is positioned on the floor. The rear northwest corner of the hall holds a single back table measuring five by five feet; it is placed three feet north‑west of the counter’s centreline, ensuring no overlap with other furniture or walls. Above each polished table and above the back table hangs a lantern suspended six feet from the floor, providing illumination for patrons. All objects are fixed in place except the wooden latch on the entrance door, which can be moved to open or close the hall. The layout preserves the medieval tavern’s authentic spatial arrangement with stone walls, creaking floors, and ample room for movement around the counter and hearth). Preserve its architecture, fixed fixtures, entrances, surfaces, persistent furniture, landmarks, and background placement when those areas enter frame. Do not use <Video 1> for characters or current camera framing.  [Shot 1] Live-action cinematic, seamless continuation. Stage this segment as one continuous camera take with no cuts or cutaways; reframe only through continuous camera movement. Preserve each established handheld or movable prop as one distinct physical object; interactions must not duplicate, merge, or substitute containers or other props.

